dekaf2 provides CMake targets:

    set(DEKAF2_INHERIT_BUILD_FLAGS ON)
    find_package(dekaf2 CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE dekaf2::dekaf2)

    # SQL convenience libraries (link the one matching an enabled backend):
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE dekaf2::ksql2)     # mysql / postgres / freetds
    target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE dekaf2::ksqlite)   # sqlite

DEKAF2_INHERIT_BUILD_FLAGS, set before find_package, gives your targets the
C++ standard and build flags dekaf2 was built with. The dekaf2 headers need
that C++ standard, and the targets do not carry it.

dekaf2 is a static framework library: by default it comes with everything it
offers, and the link of your program takes what the program uses. Features
only leave a dependency out of the build:

    sqlite       KSQLite
    yaml         YAML support
    useragent    User-Agent parser (needs yaml)
    http2        HTTP/2
    http3        HTTP/3 (QUIC through ngtcp2)
    compression  LZMA, Zstd and Brotli
    zip          ZIP archives
    webview      KWebApp on the system webview (on Linux only with the
                 WebKitGTK development packages of the system)
    mysql        MySQL / MariaDB for KSQL
    postgres     PostgreSQL for KSQL
    freetds      MS SQL Server for KSQL (FreeTDS)

Leaving dependencies out, in classic mode:

    vcpkg install dekaf2[core,sqlite,http2]

or in manifest mode (vcpkg.json):

    "dependencies": [
        { "name": "dekaf2", "default-features": false, "features": [ "sqlite", "http2" ] }
    ]

List every feature with its description any time:

    vcpkg search dekaf2
